WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.840
The phrase "life imitates art" is one that gets&nbsp;
thrown around often in the United States. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.040
in places like North and South Korea, this&nbsp;
saying is often a frightening reality ... and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.320
it doesn't get any less frightening when&nbsp;
you take into account the fact that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.720
South Korean art that has been making&nbsp;
waves lately has been very, very grim.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.120
Take "Squid Game," for example. When you take&nbsp;
away all the violence and the bright colors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:29.040
Netflix's popular survival drama is essentially&nbsp;
a grim social commentary about the bottom ladder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.120
of the social rung, and the way they have to&nbsp;
suffer at the hands of people who have more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.880
power than they do. According to a disturbing&nbsp;
November 23rd news report from Radio Free Asia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.480
another version of this chilling&nbsp;
story recently became reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.840
when a man was sentenced to&nbsp;
death because of "Squid Game."

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.640
Sometime recently, a high school student from&nbsp;
North Korea was caught buying a copy of "Squid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.480
Game" that had been "smuggled," as RFA describes&nbsp;
it, into the Hermit Kingdom on a USB flash drive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.040
The student then proceeded&nbsp;
to watch the show, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.080
"with one of his best friends in class,"&nbsp;
according to RFA sources. The sources said,

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.400
"The friend told several other students, who&nbsp;
became interested, and they shared the flash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:14.320
drive with them. They were caught by the censors&nbsp;
in 109 Sangmu, who had received a tipoff."

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.960
This reportedly led to the students' arrests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.080
as well as the capture of "the smuggler" who&nbsp;
originally sold Netflix's survival game series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.480
to them. What happened next might as well have&nbsp;
been an episode of the show at its bleakest.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:31.120
The person who sold the copy of "Squid Game"&nbsp;
was sentenced to death by firing squad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.920
after authorities discovered that he brought&nbsp;
the Netflix series into the country from China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:39.760
The students also received harsh&nbsp;
punishments, ranging from years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:45.120
hard labor to life imprisonment. Reportedly,&nbsp;
the school's principal and two faculty members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.240
were also removed from their positions for&nbsp;
allowing this to happen. RFA's source said,

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.280
"It is certain that they will&nbsp;
be sent to toil in coal mines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.160
or exiled to rural parts of the country,&nbsp;
so other school teachers are all worrying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.200
that it could happen to them too if one of their&nbsp;
students is also caught up in the investigation."

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.880
All this was done in accordance with the&nbsp;
new "Elimination of Reactionary Thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.600
and Culture" law, which carries a maximum&nbsp;
penalty of death for anyone caught, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.680
"watching, keeping, or distributing&nbsp;
media from capitalist countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.200
particularly from South Korea and the US,"&nbsp;
RFA reports. Sources indicate that the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.320
"dystopian world" in which people compete for a&nbsp;
giant cash prize and face elimination by death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:31.920
resonates with North Koreans who have "risky&nbsp;
occupations and insecure positions." Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.960
North Korea has cast similar blame on its southern&nbsp;
neighbor, with one report from a propaganda site&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:43.760
in October 2021 describing "Squid Game" as a&nbsp;
"brutal" reflection of South Korean society.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.200
According to NBC, many South&nbsp;
Koreans actually do feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.200
"Squid Game" accurately portrays the&nbsp;
wealth inequality they experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.240
just not on the level that North Korea&nbsp;
says. Security analyst Jung Dunn explained,

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:00.160
"It dealt with such familiar stories of&nbsp;
debt-ridden people you come across in real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.520
life. The story stems from a deeply rooted&nbsp;
perception of how society looks at failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.000
especially individual financial failure."

